  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CDT Thu Jun 25 2026\n\nThis Afternoon: Scattered Showers in Wisconsin \n\nWeakly cyclonic/confluent flow around our departing Great Lakes\nlow coupled with moisture flowing off Lake Superior should be \nenough to pop some afternoon showers east of the Mississippi \nRiver. However, forcing is weak and instability limited mainly \nbelow -10C, so the potential for thunderstorms is rather low \n(10-20%). These diurnal showers should slowly shift east \nthrough the afternoon with deeper mixing spreading across the \nriver and wane with the loss of daytime heating in the evening.\n\nFriday & Saturday: Last Days of Seasonal Temperatures\n\nThe rest of this week features the last few days of near to \nslightly below normal temperatures before the pattern flips next\nweek. Mainly zonal flow on Friday amplifies on Saturday as \nshortwave ridging passes through, with return flow starting to \ntake shape to our west. A surface high pressure cell slides down\nacross the Great Lakes ahead of this ridge, keeping a cooler, \neasterly lower tropospheric fetch in place. However, the \nsurface flow turns southerly during the day on Saturday and sets\nthe stage for our first truly hot and humid stretch of \nsummertime weather this year.\n\nNext Week: Precipitation Potential\n\nA surge of mid-trpospheric theta-e advection--tied to the \narrival of our heat for next week--lifts north through the \nforecast area Saturday night into Sunday morning. MUCAPE \nprofiles above this layer are pushing 2000 J/kg, with linear \nshear profiles promoting organized cell structures that could \nbring a hail risk. The cap builds for the day on Sunday and how \nquickly it builds will dictate any rain potential over northern \nWisconsin in the afternoon and evening. Early indications from \nthe deterministic are favoring a drier solution for Sunday \nafternoon and we'll see if the ensembles catch up. \n\nA nearly daily parade of evening/overnight MCSs clash with the \nwestern shield of the ridge over the Dakotas and northern MN/WI \nas we go through the week. With us being on the edge of the \nsynoptic ridge, there exists ample spread in the raw ensemble \nguidance in how far south the cold pools from these MCSs can \npenetrate into the ridge before the cap wins out. As is to be \nexpected, the higher probabilities for rainfall increase as one\ntravels northward, and the mesoscale details of how these MCSs \nwill evolve will be better resolved early next week.\n\nNext Week: Summer Heat Arrives\n\nA blocking upper level ridge takes shape Sunday into Monday \nacross the eastern half of the CONUS, which will dominate our \nweather pattern for nearly all of next week (retrograding even\nfor the end of the week). Our local area will be situated on \nthe northwestern quadrant of this ridge with southerly lower \ntropospheric flow pumping Gulf moisture northward, which when \ncombined with increasing vegetation evapotranspiration, should \npush dewpoints well into the upper 60s to mid-70s areawide. \nThere are some concerns that between the higher dewpoints and \nprecipitation potential, the NBM temperature forecast is \nsomewhat overdone for Monday and beyond. There exists a 5-8 \ndegree spread between the raw/BC guidance and the NBM (which is \nat the top of the ensemble guidance envelope). However, it will \nstill be very warm and muggy no matter how your slice the \nforecast and advisories or warnings for heat are looking \nincreasingly likely for much of next week.",
